$DIR/main.p:7:45: cannot pass inner to yield (which may call the loop body at main.p:10), which keeps it after returning
    inner points into the closure inc(n) returns, built in gen's frame. inc(n)
    returns a closure, which it builds in a region of its caller's frame, gen's
    (at main.p:6); the closure lives as long as that frame (section 4.5). yield
    (which may call the loop body at main.p:10) stores f in package variable
    saved (main.p:11), so inner would outlive gen's frame.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change yield (which may call the loop body at main.p:10) to take a value or
    an owner, which it can keep: an owned[T] from new, a shared[T], or a library
    owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
